14
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:20,880
So let's answer the big question.

15
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:25,680
What is a shader and why does it make things run so much faster?

16
00:01:25,902 --> 00:01:30,080
The speed difference comes down to how the CPU and the GPU handle data.

17
00:01:30,254 --> 00:01:38,880
The CPU or the central processing unit is the main brain of your computer built to handle complex logic one task at a time.

18
00:01:39,150 --> 00:01:47,428
Think of a CPU like a master artisan, incredible at complex work, but slow if forced to build thousands basic tables one by one.

19
00:01:47,854 --> 00:01:56,560
The GPU, on the other hand, is a specialized processor built to handle massive grids of data simultaneously by running tasks in parallel.

20
00:01:56,814 --> 00:02:04,720
Think of the GPU as a mass-scale factory floor, thousands of simple workstations executing identical tasks at once.

21
00:02:05,006 --> 00:02:08,068
A shader is simply the code written to run on the GPU.

22
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:14,320
It is the single blueprint distributed to every workstation on that factory floor at the exact same moment.

23
00:02:14,670 --> 00:02:17,760
The trade-off for this speed is total isolation.

24
00:02:18,062 --> 00:02:20,960
Pixels on the GPU cannot communicate with each other.

25
00:02:21,166 --> 00:02:24,160
Your code has no idea what the pixel next to it is doing.

26
00:02:24,334 --> 00:02:36,600
The only reason these millions of isolated pixels can form a cohesive image is because every single workstation executes the exact same mathematical blueprint across a shared current

27
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:37,348
space.

28
00:02:37,710 --> 00:02:47,280
For this series, we'll be setting up our sketches in JavaScript using P5.js, but the actual shader code will be written in a dedicated language called GLSL

29
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:49,920
or OpenGL Shading Language.

30
00:02:50,126 --> 00:02:58,080
While different frameworks require different boilerplate to get a shader on the screen, the core mathematical logic you write inside GLSL

31
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:00,160
is universally transferable.

32
00:03:00,334 --> 00:03:10,400
The newer version of P5 actually has a library called P5 Strands that allows you to write your shader code in JavaScript and it translates it to the GPU behind the scenes,

33
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:15,040
and you can check out this amazing tutorial by Juan Rodriguez Garcia to learn more.

34
00:03:15,278 --> 00:03:24,324
But for this mini series, I thought that we would look at how the logic works fundamentally so that you can pick up your shader code and drop it in any environment that you choose.

35
00:03:24,686 --> 00:03:31,520
Okay, so now that we have the split between the setup code and the shader code, how do they actually talk to each other?

36
00:03:31,886 --> 00:03:38,052
To make that happen, your setup code, or in my case, my P5JS code, needs to do exactly three things.

37
00:03:38,446 --> 00:03:44,708
First, it tells the browser to use the graphics card 3D pipeline or set it to the WebGL mode.

38
00:03:45,038 --> 00:03:50,656
And then it loads the GLSL text files into memory and sends them over to the GPU.

39
00:03:50,736 --> 00:03:55,856
And lastly, it sends information over to the GPU on every single frame.

40
00:03:56,110 --> 00:04:05,536
This live data includes two things, dynamic global variables like running timer or your live mouse coordinate, and the actual geometry data

41
00:04:05,696 --> 00:04:08,256
or the shape that you want to have drawn.

42
00:04:08,462 --> 00:04:17,616
To kick off the visual pipeline, the CPU sends a list of points to the GPU and this is where your shader code takes over and processes those points into

43
00:04:17,838 --> 00:04:20,896
distinct stages across two shader files.

44
00:04:21,198 --> 00:04:29,296
The vertex shader positions our geometry in space and the fragment shader determines the final color of every single pixel.

45
00:04:29,678 --> 00:04:39,456
When working in 2D, the vertex shader is incredibly simple. The CPU only needs to send four points or vertices off the four corners of the canvas.

46
00:04:39,790 --> 00:04:44,816
Our vertex shader is then just a boilerplate that stretches a flat canvas to frame the screen.

47
00:04:44,976 --> 00:04:50,576
Because of that, we will focus most of our energy inside the fragment shader that does all the painting.

48
00:04:50,736 --> 00:04:53,856
Alright, now let's zoom into the fragment shader.

49
00:04:54,096 --> 00:05:03,536
If your canvas is the size of let's say 800 pixels by 600 pixels, the fragment shader runs exactly 480,000 times per frame.

50
00:05:03,758 --> 00:05:10,256
On a standard 1080p display, that scale increases to over 2 million individual pixels.

51
00:05:10,542 --> 00:05:19,296
Because of this massive scale, how you approach writing a fragment shader is going to be very different from how you write a standard P5JS sketch.

52
00:05:19,566 --> 00:05:29,136
Let's say when you draw a circle on a standard P5 sketch, you would write a function like an ellipse or a circle and you would give it the xy coordinate and its size.

53
00:05:29,422 --> 00:05:34,736
But in a fragment shader, you're writing your code based on a perspective of a single pixel.

54
00:05:34,990 --> 00:05:40,736
Like mentioned before, each pixel is isolated and has no idea what its neighbor is doing.

55
00:05:40,976 --> 00:05:45,764
Without a way to talk to its neighbors, it solves for its color in four quick steps.

56
00:05:45,998 --> 00:05:56,656
First, it looks up its exact XY location on the screen, then it imports external context from the CPU like time or your mouse coordinate, And then it tests its position against your code,

57
00:05:56,974 --> 00:06:02,416
asking, for example, is my specific distance from the center less than 200 pixels?

58
00:06:02,576 --> 00:06:05,056
And if so, set the color to blue.

59
00:06:05,358 --> 00:06:09,456
And lastly, it sends the finalized color value directly out to the screen.

